Phase 1: Server Setup

What I did
Built a fresh Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS virtual machine in VirtualBox to use as a lab environment for practicing server hardening and log analysis — the first step toward having something real to secure and monitor, instead of just reading about it.

Steps taken

1. Created a new VM in VirtualBox (2GB RAM, 20GB disk) and attached the Ubuntu Server 24.04.5 LTS ISO.
2. Ran through the Ubuntu installer — default networking, used the whole disk since it was a fresh VM with nothing to lose.
3. The installer didn't give me the option to enable OpenSSH server during setup (some installer flows show it, mine didn't), so I installed it manually after logging in:
   sudo apt update
   sudo apt install openssh-server -y
   
4. Checked the service and found it wasn't running yet, so I started it:
   sudo systemctl status ssh
   sudo systemctl start ssh
   
5. The default account VirtualBox/Ubuntu had me set up during install was named "vboxuser" — not something I wanted to keep long-term, so I created a proper named user with sudo (admin) privileges instead, rather than trying to rename the existing account:
   sudo adduser <username>
   sudo usermod -aG sudo <username>
   
